      "js.tl.1876.h": "Long wires, first trouble",
      "js.tl.1876.lb": "The telephone",
      "js.tl.1876.p": "Bell's telephone works well across a room. Across a country it does not. The line itself starts changing the signal. Engineers need a theory of what a wire does to a wave.",
      "js.tl.1885.h": "Heaviside writes it down",
      "js.tl.1885.lb": "Telegrapher's equations",
      "js.tl.1885.p": "Oliver Heaviside turns <span class=\"term\" tabindex=\"0\">Maxwell<span class=\"term-pop\" data-term=\"maxwell\"><span class=\"term-pop-img\"></span><span class=\"cap\">James Clerk Maxwell (1831–1879)</span></span></span>'s work into the transmission-line equations, and gives us the idea of characteristic impedance. Now the physics is known. But the math it asks for is very hard to do by hand.",
      "js.tl.1920s.h": "The math becomes a full-time job",
      "js.tl.1920s.lb": "Bell Labs long lines",
      "js.tl.1920s.p": "Carrier telephony pushes frequencies higher and lines longer. Somebody at Bell Labs must solve a complex tangent expression, with a slide rule, hundreds of times a week.",
      "js.tl.1929.h": "A field, an antenna, a stub tuner",
      "js.tl.1929.lb": "Deal Beach, NJ",
      "js.tl.1929.p": "Phillip Smith, fresh from Tufts and newly hired at the Bell Labs radio station, spends his days on a slow job that needs two people: one engineer moves a tuning stub by hand, the other reads standing-wave numbers from a test set far away and shouts them back. He had done the amateur version of this in his own backyard for years. Now it was his job.",
      "js.tl.1931.h": "A rectangular first try",
      "js.tl.1931.lb": "Smith's first chart",
      "js.tl.1931.p": "Smith draws a chart to save himself the arithmetic. It works, but he draws it on rectangular coordinates, so it can only show a small range of impedances. Infinity does not fit on paper.",
      "js.tl.1937.h": "The same idea, somewhere else",
      "js.tl.1937.lb": "Mizuhashi, Japan",
      "js.tl.1937.p": "Tosaku Mizuhashi publishes a very similar chart in Japan. The problem existed everywhere, so the solution was found more than once.",
      "js.tl.1939.h": "“Transmission Line Calculator”",
      "js.tl.1939.lb": "Published",
      "js.tl.1939.p": "Smith moves to the reflection-coefficient plane, where every passive impedance fits inside a circle of radius 1. He publishes it in Electronics, January 1939. The same year, in the Soviet Union, Amiel Volpert finds the same construction.",
      "js.tl.1944.h": "The version you know",
      "js.tl.1944.lb": "The improved chart",
      "js.tl.1944.p": "“An Improved Transmission Line Calculator” adds the outer wavelength scales and the radial rulers for SWR and return loss. This is the chart that is still printed today.",
      "js.tl.1969.h": "Electronic Applications of the Smith Chart",
      "js.tl.1969.lb": "The book",
      "js.tl.1969.p": "Thirty years after inventing it, Smith writes the main book about his own chart. He retires from Bell Labs soon after.",
      "js.tl.now.h": "Still on the screen",
      "js.tl.now.lb": "Every VNA",
      "js.tl.now.p": "Computers made the calculation free, and changed nothing about the chart. Open any vector network analyser, any RF simulator, any antenna datasheet: the circles are there, doing the one job software still cannot do for you — showing you which way to move.",
      "js.tlc_sub": "IMPEDANCE  OR  ADMITTANCE  COORDINATES",
      "js.tlc_title": "TRANSMISSION LINE CALCULATOR",
      "js.voltage_now": "VOLTAGE RIGHT NOW",
      "meta.title": "Folding infinity into a circle — the Smith chart · hkk.fyi",
      "meta.description": "Two engineers stuck in a field in 1929, and the piece of graph paper one of them drew to escape. A plain-English, play-with-everything guide to the Smith chart — no RF background needed.",
      "meta.og:title": "Folding infinity into a circle — the Smith chart",
      "meta.og:description": "A 1939 piece of graph paper that computers could not kill. Seven live figures, no RF background needed.",
      "meta.og:image:alt": "Folding infinity into a circle — a Smith chart beside the article title.",
